# Project architecture

Stack: Next.js App Router, React 19, TypeScript, Tailwind 4. Import with `@/`.

Read `node_modules/next/dist/docs/` before using a Next.js API. This version differs from older App Router docs.

## Where code lives

- `app/` — routes only: `page.tsx`, `layout.tsx`, `loading.tsx`, `error.tsx`, `not-found.tsx`, and route handlers. A page composes features. It does not own UI markup or data logic.
- `components/ui/` — presentational primitives with no domain knowledge (`Button`, `Input`, `Dialog`).
- `components/layout/` — shell pieces shared across routes (`Header`, `Footer`, `Container`).
- `features/<name>/` — one product area per folder: `components/`, `hooks/`, `actions.ts`, `types.ts`.
- `lib/` — pure helpers, formatters, and clients. No React.
- `hooks/` and `types/` — only code used by more than one feature.

## Dependencies

- A feature may import `components/ui`, `components/layout`, and `lib`.
- Features do not import each other. Share through `lib` or `components`.
- Colocate code in the feature that owns it. Move it to `components/`, `hooks/`, or `lib/` only when a second consumer exists.
- Create a folder when the first real file needs it.

## Server and client

- Server Components are the default.
- Add `"use client"` only on the smallest leaf that needs state, effects, or browser APIs.
- Fetching and mutations live in the feature (`actions.ts` or a server module), not in UI components.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD — page owns markup and data
export default async function Page() {
  const items = await db.list();
  return <ul>{items.map((item) => <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>)}</ul>;
}

// ✅ GOOD — page composes the feature
import { ItemList } from "@/features/items/components/ItemList";

export default function Page() {
  return <ItemList />;
}
```
